WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.040
Hello everyone. Nice to see you.

2
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:06.120
Really exciting to be here helping to kick off day two of DevCon.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.040
And I'm impressed by the number of people in the room today.

4
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.520
You have made it through the weather, the tube strikes and you're here.

5
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.520
I know I had my own battle to get here this morning as well.

6
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.520
I'm Marc Sloan, I'm a member of the product team here at Tessl, and I'm here

7
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.760
today at a developer focused conference to talk about non developers.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.120
Now, I don't know if there are any other non developers in the room here today.

9
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.480
I'm thinking yeah there we go.

10
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.720
Product managers, designers executives you know

11
00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.880
the people who traditionally wouldn't touch code.

12
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.720
Although I think we're all we're all turning into developers these days right.

13
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.160
This talk

14
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.960
is about the work that they create, but also it is for everyone, rest assured,

15
00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.160
because as we talk increasingly about the context

16
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.160
that we give agents, coding agents,

17
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.960
a lot of that, the origination of that context

18
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.600
comes from non developers, comes from product and design teams.

19
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.280
And so what I'm going to talk about today is the creation of that context,

20
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.080
the challenges we face in exposing that to our coding agents and their harnesses.

21
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.080
And what solutions to those challenges might look like.

22
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.440
Now, over the last 24 hours, the conversation at Dev Con has been

23
00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.960
pretty much about one thing, and that's giving agents the right context.

24
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.680
I think it's been super interesting working in this area

25
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.400
over the last 6 to 12 months, and we've seen the methodology

26
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.200
we use to do that, evolving from spec driven

27
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.640
development, where we right up front all the context that the agents

28
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.760
are going to need in order to get the task done

29
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.560
to context engineering, which is kind of where we are just now,

30
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.880
and how that's evolving into harness engineering.

31
00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.800
And the tools are maturing really fast in this area, but a lot of

32
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.040
it has been focused on context that originates and lives within the code base.

33
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.040
And the natural form that that context has been taking

34
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.240
recently has been in the form of skills.

35
00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.640
Now, I don't think I need to convince anybody here of the

36
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.040
the value of skills and how powerful they are.

37
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.440
There was a great quote in one of the talks yesterday

38
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.640
about how the simplest solution is often the right one,

39
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.960
and the fact that the markdown file is so flexible and adaptable

40
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.160
makes it the natural place to be able to put code based context.

41
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.000
I think another powerful aspect of it is the fact that that context

42
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.080
can live alongside the code that it's describing, that it lives

43
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.120
within the repo, that it's managed, it's versioned and can be subject

44
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.320
to evaluations and tests and all that good stuff that comes with the harness.

45
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.520
And we're still figuring out how to manage that kind of context.

46
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.320
Right?

47
00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.040
All the stuff we've been talking about with harness engineering,

48
00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.360
the features that you see in the Tessl product, are all about figuring out

49
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.880
how we turn skills and context into code.

50
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.840
So to kick us off

51
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.640
and to kick off this conversation about product

52
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.560
and design context, I'm going to walk through a typical example,

53
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.200
the kind of thing I'm sure everybody in this room is familiar to.

54
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.720
Everybody in this room.

55
00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.040
We're going to describe a scenario here where we've got a company that has a web

56
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.520
app product, and a customer has asked the product manager

57
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.640
to put an export button on the dashboard in their product.

58
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.480
Now, the export feature was something that was hidden behind the settings menu,

59
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.840
and there was a couple of layers deep, so it was very difficult to find.

60
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.840
Customer wasn't aware that it existed, and requested

61
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.840
this to be in a more prominent position.

62
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.920
On the surface, this seems like the perfect

63
00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.160
kind of job to give to an Agent a Linear ticket that describes it.

64
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.640
Put a figma design in there that shows where the button should go

65
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.640
and what it should look like.

66
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.160
All that good stuff

67
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.560
fired off to your agents, have it produce a PR job done, customers happy.

68
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.640
The feature got delivered.

69
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.640
The customer tried it out and said, it's great.

70
00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.680
So cool.

71
00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.440
This is the world we live in right now.

72
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.080
We can just send these things off to agents.

73
00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.080
And of course, the agent wasn't building this blind.

74
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.240
It had access to a bunch of skills that had been developed in advance

75
00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.440
to instruct the agent on how it should be, thinking about the architecture

76
00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.920
of the code, about the front end components that live

77
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.120
within that, the testing and CI requirements,

78
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.560
and how to make use of the API to get the data.

79
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.640
And of course, this is a cutting edge state of the art company.

80
00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.560
So they're using Tessl to manage all of this stuff.

81
00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.840
So problem solved.

82
00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.680
Why do we need to be thinking about product and design context?

83
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.360
Well in this scenario the agent got one thing wrong.

84
00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:06.400
It actually ended up using a generic react button component

85
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.840
that lived within the code base,

86
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.040
and not the more specific export button components.

87
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.800
That was the right choice here,

88
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.800
and this might seem like a trivial error,

89
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.760
but encoded within that export button component

90
00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.520
are the accessibility requirements that this organization adheres to.

91
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.480
Interaction patterns that make sense for this kind of async,

92
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.080
the async operation of this kind of button.

93
00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.960
And this was all missed by the agent in creating this.

94
00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.720
And what's challenging here is that as far as the harness was concerned,

95
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.040
everything went smoothly.

96
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.920
The agent got a ticket handed off to it, did the job, created a PR

97
00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.280
that passed, and the feature went out to the customer.

98
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.720
The valuations that were set up on the skills

99
00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.480
and the other aspects of the harness all seem to be working well,

100
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.600
but the product and design context was missing

101
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.600
from the agent in this particular situation.

102
00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.800
And this can have real world consequences because while the customer was happy

103
00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.920
out of the bat in this situation, it might be that down the line.

104
00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.960
The app then ends up failing an accessibility audit

105
00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.920
that delays a critical deal and that kind of stuff.

106
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:17.880
So we might

107
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.000
ask ourselves, well, how should the agents have learned about that?

108
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.640
How can it find the right context to do it?

109
00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.400
Well, the context was in the design system in Figma this whole time.

110
00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.720
It turns out that the design system team had thought this stuff through

111
00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.520
and already thought about how this button should be created, how

112
00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.480
it should be designed, and how the interaction should work with it.

113
00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:47.200
In fact, the Figma design that was in that original linear ticket did correctly use

114
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:51.320
the export button design component, but the agent just didn't pick this up.

115
00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.800
It used the Figma design as a visual treatment, and then from that,

116
00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.640
inferred that it should be using a generic button that it customized.

117
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.440
So as we think about building agent harnesses,

118
00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.440
as we've been doing for the last 24 hours in this conference,

119
00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.560
we might ask ourselves, where does product and design context fit into this?

120
00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.640
Now, the way I think about Agent Harnesses is that it's a layer that wraps

121
00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:24.600
the code base itself, and that layer contains skills, evaluations,

122
00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.040
hooks, policies, all this other stuff that we're working on at the moment.

123
00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.680
And we're using tools like Tessl to manage

124
00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.040
the product and design context.

125
00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.960
I see existing in this layer surrounding the harness, at least right now.

126
00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.080
And it's not just that it's a layer outside of the harness,

127
00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.800
but this data doesn't live in the code base. By design.

128
00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.680
It lives in the third party tools that product managers and designers

129
00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.680
and so on are using day to day,

130
00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:53.880
every day.

131
00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:58.040
Context is getting created in these third party tools, and the code base

132
00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.160
is blind to it, and therefore the agent and the agent harness as well.

133
00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.560
And it makes sense that it lives in those tools, because that's the tools

134
00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.040
where product managers and designers are more are spending their time.

135
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.240
Designers are going to continue to work in Figma

136
00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:15.040
because it has a canvas and a great set of tools for creating designs.

137
00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.000
PMS will keep working in linear and notion, and more customer data

138
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.160
is going to continue to live in CRM,

139
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.120
and for as long as

140
00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.880
that keeps happening, we're going to have important contexts that agents

141
00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.960
need in order to build the right things and do the right job

142
00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:34.080
living outside the code base, and not just the context.

143
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.160
Agents need to get the job done, but also the context.

144
00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.760
We need to understand whether the job got done correctly.

145
00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.000
So challenge one is the fact that product and design context

146
00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.000
is important to agents, but it lives outside the code base.

147
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.760
If we go back to our example, let's imagine

148
00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.840
the team has picked up on this and decided to try and

149
00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.240
bring that context into the code base in order to solve this problem.

150
00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:00.000
They've set up storybook.

151
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
They've gone through the design system in Figma and made sure

152
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.760
all of the design components that are there are well represented in storybook,

153
00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.560
with the appropriate documentation and interaction patterns and so on.

154
00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.920
This means the next time a request like this comes in,

155
00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.320
the agent picks up the right component correctly and gets the job done.

156
00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.600
Perfect.

157
00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.040
This is all working, but the world keeps evolving

158
00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.840
and the product and design context isn't static.

159
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.800
You can imagine in this situation something like this where a new deal comes

160
00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.560
in, and with this new deal comes a new set of accessibility requirements.

161
00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.920
Let's say let's say it's a government agency.

162
00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.360
The PM picks this up and

163
00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.760
produces a notion document that outlines what those requirements are.

164
00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.760
The the design team update Figma and the design components

165
00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.800
there to make sure that they are fully compliant.

166
00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.800
And while all that's going on, the code base is falling

167
00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.440
increasingly out of sync with what's going on in product and design land,

168
00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:01.160
and the agent is still producing features using the outdated design system.

169
00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.320
And so for as long as we accept that product and design context

170
00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.520
is going to live outside the code base, then we're going to have

171
00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.360
this problem of the two falling out of sync with each other.

172
00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.960
And I've talked about this in one direction so far,

173
00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:22.040
with the product and design context evolving as the code stays the same.

174
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.360
But the opposite is also true, especially as we have more and more agents

175
00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.040
being thrown into the code base

176
00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:32.280
and building new components from scratch and updating existing components.

177
00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:36.800
We're also going to find ourselves in the situation where the app itself

178
00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.560
has a whole bunch of components the design team know nothing about,

179
00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:41.520
and so we need to be able to move the context

180
00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.320
in the other direction for them as well.

181
00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:47.600
Now, in all of

182
00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.600
this, you might be thinking, isn't this a solved problem?

183
00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:53.600
Should all of these tools have MCP servers today?

184
00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:58.360
Isn't it just a case of connecting the agent to the MCP server and we're done?

185
00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.360
That's true to an extent, but

186
00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.000
the fact that we need to have this live connection to third party product

187
00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.120
and design context also introduces a set of new challenges, especially as we

188
00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.400
now start to think about the agent harness

189
00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:13.360
as being something

190
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.400
that's managed and controlled, and we want to be improving all of the time.

191
00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:21.880
We now have a live connection that we need to maintain and work around the rate

192
00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:24.880
limits of that imposes cost constraints

193
00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.960
and the third party availability.

194
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.880
We also get into a situation where the evals

195
00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.400
and things like that, that we're setting up on.

196
00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.440
The skills and the context in the code base

197
00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.200
is now reliant on third party context that's constantly changing.

198
00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:42.320
That doesn't necessarily have a canonical source of truth.

199
00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.120
And of course, that's the problem with product and design context.

200
00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.760
It is constantly up to date.

201
00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:50.440
That's never really a definition of done there.

202
00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.920
It can be in an incomplete state by virtue of its nature.

203
00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.400
And so if we're building harnesses that are reliant on that,

204
00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.200
we have to be very careful about how we make that work.

205
00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.280
So let's

206
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.280
pause for a moment to reflect on what we've established here.

207
00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.680
I'm saying that for agents to work effectively and for the harnesses

208
00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.760
that use them to work effectively, they need to take into account product

209
00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:15.920
and design context.

210
00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.680
And this, by its nature, lives outside of the code base.

211
00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.320
It, by its nature, falls out of sync with the code base over time,

212
00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.360
and that trying to maintain a live connection between the agent

213
00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.800
and that context introduces a whole set of new challenges

214
00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:35.800
that we have to deal with.

215
00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.880
Now, everything I'm talking about

216
00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.960
might seem theoretical,

217
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.680
or, you know, we're still just trying to get a head around context in the code

218
00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.680
base around skills and how we manage skills and so on.

219
00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.560
Is this really such a big deal at the moment?

220
00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:52.880
Well, everything I've described

221
00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.640
so far are real challenges that I've faced in my life.

222
00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.240
Prior to Tessl, when I was working at Figma on their dev mode product.

223
00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:04.280
For those who don't know, dev

224
00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:09.240
mode is a feature in Figma, which allows designers to hand designs off

225
00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.440
to developers human developers in this case, back in

226
00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:15.240
back in the Stone age, these were the tools we were using

227
00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:20.920
and it gives the developer lots of great context about the designs

228
00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.440
that they can use in manually creating the code to implement those designs.

229
00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.360
And over the last couple of years, we've seen that feature

230
00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:32.880
evolve into Figma, MCP server, the live Connection.

231
00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:33.720
Right.

232
00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:38.600
And what we're doing here is just taking that exact same design context,

233
00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.680
but giving it directly to an agent via the MCP server.

234
00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:45.680
And it was in building this that we started

235
00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.680
to see all of those problems that I've been describing.

236
00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.760
We would see agents picking up the designs

237
00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.680
and doing a fantastic job in creating front end code

238
00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:59.600
that looked just like the design that functioned as the designer intended.

239
00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.960
But the developers would look at the code and it would have made up

240
00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.400
a bunch of components when they otherwise existed,

241
00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.160
or used them in the wrong way, or completely missed that

242
00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.200
there was a design system that it should have been working towards.

243
00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.120
These were real problems that I encountered with customers every day.

244
00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:17.680
And so we started.

245
00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.200
We realized quite quickly that we needed to find a way to bridge

246
00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.600
that product and design context gap with what agents were doing.

247
00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:29.400
And to that end, an early feature we started working on to address

248
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:31.520
this was Figma Code Connect product.

249
00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.560
And here is a tool that explicitly

250
00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:39.240
allowed design system teams to connect the design components

251
00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:42.880
in that design system with their equivalent in the code base.

252
00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:48.360
Now I don't want to go into and code connect too much.

253
00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:53.320
The point here is that these aren't new problems.

254
00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:56.400
These were problems over the last several years that product

255
00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.880
teams and design teams and devs are dealing with day to day.

256
00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.360
Already.

257
00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:04.760
We were already dealing with a product and design context to agent

258
00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.280
harness problem before we even defined half of those terms,

259
00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:12.680
and struggling to find the right solution for that.

260
00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:18.360
And it's from working on that over the last couple of years.

261
00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:23.360
And now with my role at all, thinking about how those kinds of things apply

262
00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.440
to harness engineering, that I think there are some useful lessons

263
00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.760
we can take from that as we think about solutions to that problem.

264
00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.200
The first lesson is that

265
00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.960
in building this Code Connect tool, we were aware that there was that

266
00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:41.520
that connection between product and design context and what's in the code

267
00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:44.920
base required dedicated maintenance in enterprises.

268
00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.480
There are entire teams whose sole job is to make sure that that connection

269
00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.800
is up to date and is makes sense,

270
00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.800
and that there is equivalence between the two.

271
00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:59.720
And one of the reasons those teams exist, and that maintenance is required,

272
00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:03.040
is because the design context and the product context

273
00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:06.320
is constantly falling out of sync with the code base.

274
00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.480
Both are evolving at their own pace for their own reasons,

275
00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:13.360
and it requires dedicated effort to keep the two reconciled.

276
00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:16.680
And one of the learnings was that sometimes

277
00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.120
it's not desirable, actually, to keep the two completely in sync.

278
00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.240
There's an acceptance that they will, of course, have to be out of sync because

279
00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:27.080
of the pace of design work and product work and dev work and so on.

280
00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:30.520
And it's the job of this team just to make sure the status quo

281
00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.760
is functional and working and to keep track of that over time.

282
00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:37.560
And I think that's broadly true not just of design and code context,

283
00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:39.880
but product and design context in general.

284
00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.160
The third lesson, I think, is interesting in that

285
00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.000
as someone working in product management on this product,

286
00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:51.600
I was also very much aware of the the business

287
00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:54.600
and the structural constraints around the tools we were building.

288
00:16:55.800 --> 00:17:00.000
Figma as an organization was very protective of its design system IP.

289
00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:01.600
It didn't just want to give away the keys

290
00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:03.920
to the house, and so we had to be very careful

291
00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:07.480
about finding the right balance between supporting agents

292
00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:11.120
and the developers using them, but also not giving everything away

293
00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:14.000
and destroying the moat that they've built over time.

294
00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.600
And I think that's true across all of these product

295
00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:19.600
and design SaaS providers, not just Figma.

296
00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:26.600
So as I spend my days currently thinking about harness

297
00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:29.120
engineering and how we make sure that agents

298
00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.320
have all the information they need to do a great job for developers,

299
00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:34.560
and I take

300
00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.680
on board these lessons that I've learned from my experience in trying to solve this

301
00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.680
in other organizations.

302
00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:43.360
I see maybe three directions that the harness

303
00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.480
can take in order to solve this.

304
00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.480
And if we've got a bit of time, there's a sneaky fourth one as well.

305
00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:52.360
The first is that we can see these tools

306
00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:56.120
perhaps opening up, and we're already seeing this.

307
00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:00.680
The number of these third party tools that have MCP servers just now is exploding.

308
00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:05.240
And we're also seeing them expand with their own APIs and cliffs and so on.

309
00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.440
I think you still do see restrictions and rate limits in place.

310
00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:11.880
And we're also seeing this surface being an opportunity

311
00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.520
for those organizations to create new monetization pathways as well.

312
00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:19.560
So I don't think the solution here is as straightforward as all

313
00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.760
the data will just become available for everything.

314
00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:25.000
But I do think this is going to get us some of the way there.

315
00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:28.520
And it's something where we're in the middle of at the moment.

316
00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.520
Another option is the

317
00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:37.120
idea that there is an agent that sits between the third party

318
00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:42.480
product and design context, and the harness agent in particular.

319
00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:45.360
The lesson I learned was about this connection,

320
00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.920
requiring dedicated maintenance and pruning over time.

321
00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:53.240
And increasingly we did see those teams using agents to a system.

322
00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:57.320
So it's entirely reasonable that they start to get to a place

323
00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.560
where they can automate a lot of that work.

324
00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:03.280
And I think as we think as well about the harness itself

325
00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:07.040
acquiring its own agent in order to build and manage that harness,

326
00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:10.920
you know, Tessl's IO agent, check it out.

327
00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.640
We can see the role that

328
00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.960
those kinds of agents might have expanding to cover,

329
00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.400
not just managing the context

330
00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:23.280
within the code base, but also the context that lives outside of it as well.

331
00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.240
And finally,

332
00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:30.160
another option here is that this context

333
00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:33.600
that lives outside the code base and is unmanaged and versioned,

334
00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:38.360
perhaps just gets swallowed by the repo, and we'll see it making inroads into it,

335
00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:41.800
and then being able to be managed by the harness directly.

336
00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:46.280
And I think what's interesting with this is that there are some challenges here.

337
00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:49.800
Certainly we're going to have to still meat products

338
00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:52.960
and designers and other non developers where they're at,

339
00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.760
because designers are still going to want to design on a canvas regardless

340
00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.760
of where that data ends up living.

341
00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:01.640
Likewise with CRMs and things like that.

342
00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:03.600
So we need to meet them where they're at.

343
00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.800
But it could be that there's just still so much value

344
00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:13.280
in having this context within the harness, that we see an organic pull of that data

345
00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:14.480
into a place where it can be

346
00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:18.400
managed and versioned and evaluated and all this great harness stuff.

347
00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:20.480
I also think what's

348
00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:25.560
interesting about this direction is it also calls to the fact

349
00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:31.000
that we as non developers are increasingly looking like developers.

350
00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:35.720
In fact, there are several talks yesterday and today specifically about this topic

351
00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:38.840
and about product managers and designers and so on, creating

352
00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.880
PRS and actively contributing to the code base.

353
00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:46.800
This might be the impetus for us to fold the kind of context

354
00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.800
that we're generating day by day into the repo.

355
00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.320
And with that in mind,

356
00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:56.400
what I want to do is go back and revisit

357
00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:01.920
our example from earlier and think about the situation where

358
00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.560
perhaps a developer wasn't involved in this at all.

359
00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:11.400
We can imagine a PM like myself had that conversation with a customer.

360
00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:16.400
We transcribed that conversation using Granola, fed that into Claude in Cowork.

361
00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:21.240
I have my own set of skills there that are able to look through transcripts

362
00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:26.240
like that and identify features from them, turn those features into linear tickets.

363
00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:30.400
I have a skill that encodes my roadmap and make sure that this fits my roadmap

364
00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:35.280
and has the right priority, and one thing to call out here is that I,

365
00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:41.120
as a non developer, might be developing these skills outside of the code base.

366
00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:42.320
These aren't versioned.

367
00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:44.800
These don't have these aren't accessible to my harness.

368
00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:46.720
They're not aware of them.

369
00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:49.440
So we need to make sure that

370
00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.320
this might be the first way that we bring

371
00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:55.440
some of that context into the code base to manage that kind of thing.

372
00:21:55.880 --> 00:22:00.080
And of course, here Claude turns the feature

373
00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:02.640
from the granola transcript into a linear ticket

374
00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:04.320
that then fires off that flow from earlier.

375
00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.600
The feature gets built and the customer is happy.

376
00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:10.160
Except

377
00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:13.200
what the PM didn't know is that there's a reason

378
00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.440
this export feature was hidden behind various settings.

379
00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.120
Menu.

380
00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:20.480
Because of some architectural decision that was made sometime in the past,

381
00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:25.000
that made exporting the customer's data a hugely expensive task.

382
00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:26.960
And therefore, we didn't want this to be something

383
00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.680
that was widely available and operating with high bandwidth.

384
00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.000
This causes a PR with a huge number of changes, and

385
00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:40.240
all of this is invisible to the PM, who's happily tapping away in their computer,

386
00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:41.520
clapping themselves in the back

387
00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:44.680
for the fact that they're able to push PRS and make features by themselves.

388
00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:50.760
And what's missing here is I know personally from my own experience

389
00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:55.440
managing technical teams, when I'm deciding which features to work

390
00:22:55.440 --> 00:23:00.080
on, I massively value the feedback and conversation

391
00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:03.080
that I'm having with my team to let me know.

392
00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:03.800
Help me understand

393
00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:07.120
the technical effort that goes into creating some of these features.

394
00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:09.560
That helps me understand the technical debt

395
00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:12.560
that we might be creating as a result of this.

396
00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:15.800
And it can be just as valuable for a product manager to know

397
00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:19.400
when not to build something than to just look at a backlog

398
00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:23.520
as its do list that we just need to throw agents at and work through.

399
00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.960
And so another challenge here is that so far,

400
00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:31.720
I've been talking about bringing product and design context to coding agents.

401
00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:35.720
But we also need to think about this the other way around, and bringing

402
00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:40.000
coding context to the kind of decision making that's happening

403
00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:43.000
outside of the code base, but that's still affects it.

404
00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:46.280
And so one way this might evolve is

405
00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:50.880
we keep talking about coding agents and the harnesses that enable them,

406
00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:54.600
but perhaps it's going to be more like each of the different roles within

407
00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:58.160
a product team will have their own agent and their own harness.

408
00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:01.080
And these harnesses are going to have to talk to each other.

409
00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:04.080
And maybe this is what the solution ends up looking like.

410
00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:09.000
Now, it's not clear which direction these things are going to go

411
00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:10.720
in, and we're obviously going to see a lot of progress

412
00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:13.720
being made on this front in the next 6 to 12 months.

413
00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:17.120
I think the thing that's clear to me is that product

414
00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:20.960
and design context is critically important for coding agents.

415
00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:24.000
And as we work on harnesses for coding agents, certainly

416
00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.000
over the next couple of months, we need to take that into account

417
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.440
and figure out the best way

418
00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:31.440
to overcome some of the challenges that we've talked about today.

419
00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:34.520
I'm sure folks in this room are actively working on this.

420
00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:37.920
I know I am as well, and I'm really excited to be working in this area.

421
00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:41.280
And with that, I'd like to say thank you so much.

422
00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:45.400
Being a great audience, I'm happy to stick around and answer a few questions.

423
00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:52.800
Awesome.

424
00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.680
Thank you very much. Right.

425
00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:55.600
I'm going to try these.

426
00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:59.080
I'm going to try and run through these tables and get get the mic to people.

427
00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.080
If anyone's got a question please raise your hand.

428
00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:07.160
Any questions?

429
00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:09.280
Thanks.

430
00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:10.680
Thanks for the talk.

431
00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:14.240
My first question comes in if you feed

432
00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:18.120
in more context in addition to what we're already feeding in.

433
00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.160
Like where do you draw the line and how do you group them?

434
00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:25.040
When do you when do you feed in a bit of context that's needed?

435
00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:28.480
I remember there was some board activities

436
00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:33.040
at my workplace where they were asking what all should be added to the code base.

437
00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:36.800
So there was this whole wash list of things, including design,

438
00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:39.360
and everybody wanted to get everything in the code base

439
00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:43.160
because they want to feed all of that, like, you know, slack conversations, teams

440
00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:46.320
conversations, your email, conversations with your client.

441
00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:48.840
It's like, where do you draw? Where would you draw that line?

442
00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.000
Because obviously you need context.

443
00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:52.960
But then context is king.

444
00:25:52.960 --> 00:25:56.640
But then which part of it is it's it's quite subjective as well right.

445
00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:57.280
Yeah.

446
00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:59.800
So some, some some of your thoughts on that.

447
00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:00.440
Yeah.

448
00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.600
It's you know, where do you draw the line in terms of adding context to the agent

449
00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:07.160
when I'm advocating not just for adding code

450
00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:09.200
based context, but also product and design context,

451
00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:13.160
which is likely even larger than what's in the code base.

452
00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:16.560
I mean, I think this is the core problem of context engineering and harness

453
00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:19.440
engineering in general, even if we just remove the product

454
00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:23.680
and design context question for now, it's already a huge challenge to figure out

455
00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:27.560
how to encode all of the contexts that might live within a repo

456
00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:31.880
and its architecture and its norms, and so on, into something succinct enough

457
00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:35.640
that's relevant enough for the agent to do the right job.

458
00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:40.040
And part of the reason we are setting up things like evaluation architectures

459
00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:45.200
and so on, is so that we can actively work to get that context down into

460
00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:47.600
just the right size, but also make sure that that context

461
00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:50.760
is actually doing a good job to evaluate and so on.

462
00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:52.640
We're going to have the exact same problem,

463
00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:54.440
I think, with product and design context.

464
00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:58.640
In fact, that makes it harder because it's I think, as I've said today,

465
00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:02.200
by virtue, it's messier, there's more of it.

466
00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:03.960
It's in lots of different places.

467
00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.240
And so

468
00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:08.520
I think we just need to well, for now, we need to apply the same kind of tools.

469
00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:12.240
We need to figure out which design context is signal

470
00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:15.960
versus noise, and perhaps have agents to help us decipher that.

471
00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:19.760
We need to shrink that down into formats that are enough to either

472
00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:23.560
live as skills in the code base, or maybe there's some other methodology

473
00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:27.280
that we shrink this to and have that be accessible to the agent.

474
00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:31.080
And then we need a set of evals to make sure that our agent

475
00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:34.760
is actually being boosted by that context and isn't being overwhelmed.

476
00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:38.160
So the short answer is, I think we need to apply

477
00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:42.720
all of the same principles with code base context to product and design context

478
00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:45.720
with that extra layer of difficulty that comes with that, because

479
00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:47.160
of it being external

480
00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.160
trial and error and evaluate.

481
00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:53.120
Exactly.

482
00:27:53.120 --> 00:27:55.000
Probably got time for one more question if there's

483
00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.000
any other questions in the room.

484
00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:02.320
If not, thank you very much.

485
00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:05.320
Please give it up for Mark again.

486
00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:08.440
Okay.

487
00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:10.360
We've got just under ten minutes till the next session
